# Semicolons join two clauses that can each stand as a sentence, and
# the reader has to find where the first clause ends. A clause is a
# subject and its verb, such as `the tests pass`. A period puts that
# boundary where the reader expects one. When the second clause states
# a cause, a result, or a contrast, `because`, `so`, or `but` names the
# link that the semicolon left to the reader. The rule flags a word with
# a semicolon glued to it.
#
# A closing paren before the mark passes, so a call in a comment
# (`foo();`) stays quiet. Loop headers in source comments
# (`for (i = 0; i < n; i++)`) still fire, so the rule sits at
# Recommended. Harper does not tag a digit glued to a semicolon as a
# number, so `5;` passes either way and NUM stays out of the class.
#
# Splitting a contrastive negation
# (`This isn't a cache; it's a ledger`) at the semicolon silences
# ProseContrastiveNegation, which reads one sentence at a time, but the
# denied framing remains. Drop the first clause instead of splitting it.
#
# It hit 742 lines in the measure.py corpus, and 16 of 20 sampled hits
# joined two clauses. The misses were a phrase with no verb after the
# semicolon (`; for testing`), a list item that ends in a semicolon, and
# a media type (`text/html; charset=utf-8`).
expr main [([NOUN, VERB, ADJ, ADV, PRON, PROPN, DET];)]
let message "Semicolon. End the sentence with a period, or join the clauses with `because`, `so`, or `but`."
let description "Flags a semicolon after a word, where it joins two clauses that could each stand as a sentence."
let becomes ""
let kind "Punctuation"
allows "Call foo(); the hook returns."
allows "The value is 5; the limit is 10."
